1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,273
This is the southern Amazon region of
South America, mapped from satellite data.

2
00:00:07,723 --> 00:00:12,979
The coloured areas are places where tree
cover that existed in the year two

3
00:00:12,979 --> 00:00:18,586
thousand was lost between two thousand and
one and two thousand and twenty-four.

4
00:00:19,036 --> 00:00:20,758
Yellow is early.

5
00:00:21,208 --> 00:00:23,028
Red is recent.

6
00:00:23,478 --> 00:00:27,107
Each pixel covers about thirty metres of
ground.

7
00:00:27,557 --> 00:00:32,261
Today we will use maps like this one to
ask a simple question.

8
00:00:32,711 --> 00:00:36,535
How much forest is being lost, and how do
we know?

9
00:00:38,035 --> 00:00:42,348
Forests cover four point one four billion
hectares.

10
00:00:42,798 --> 00:00:45,742
About one third of the land on Earth.

11
00:00:46,192 --> 00:00:51,765
At least one point one eight billion
hectares of that is classified as primary

12
00:00:51,765 --> 00:00:52,265
forest.

13
00:00:52,715 --> 00:00:58,228
These figures come from the Food and
Agriculture Organization's Global Forest

14
00:00:58,228 --> 00:01:03,383
Resources Assessment, published in October
two thousand and twenty-five.

15
00:01:03,833 --> 00:01:07,853
It covers two hundred and thirty-six
countries and areas.

16
00:01:09,353 --> 00:01:12,542
Mapping forests from space is not new.

17
00:01:12,992 --> 00:01:19,091
NASA's first Landsat satellite launched on
the twenty-third of July, nineteen

18
00:01:19,091 --> 00:01:20,042
seventy-two.

19
00:01:20,492 --> 00:01:25,782
Since then, Landsat satellites have
continuously acquired images of the

20
00:01:25,782 --> 00:01:27,347
Earth's land surface.

21
00:01:27,797 --> 00:01:34,344
Today Europe's Sentinel-2 satellites add
images at ten-metre resolution, revisiting

22
00:01:34,344 --> 00:01:37,341
the same ground about every five days.

23
00:01:37,791 --> 00:01:41,322
A map is only as good as its definition.

24
00:01:41,772 --> 00:01:47,636
In the global forest change dataset from
the University of Maryland, tree cover

25
00:01:47,636 --> 00:01:50,680
means vegetation taller than five metres.

26
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:56,932
Loss means a stand-replacement
disturbance, a change from forest to non-

27
00:01:56,932 --> 00:01:57,496
forest.

28
00:01:57,946 --> 00:02:00,010
That definition matters.

29
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:06,114
It counts what satellites can see, which
is not always the same as what a country

30
00:02:06,114 --> 00:02:07,510
calls deforestation.

31
00:02:09,010 --> 00:02:14,313
There is no single number for
deforestation, because there is no single

32
00:02:14,313 --> 00:02:14,985
question.

33
00:02:15,435 --> 00:02:21,262
The FAO assessment is compiled with
national correspondents from one hundred

34
00:02:21,262 --> 00:02:24,099
and ninety-seven countries and areas.

35
00:02:24,549 --> 00:02:30,269
Global Forest Watch measures tree cover
loss from satellites, and that includes

36
00:02:30,269 --> 00:02:31,355
loss from fire.

37
00:02:31,805 --> 00:02:38,892
And Brazil's space agency, INPE, runs a
programme called PRODES that measures

38
00:02:38,892 --> 00:02:43,402
deforestation in the Brazilian Legal
Amazon only.

39
00:02:43,852 --> 00:02:49,213
Those numbers should never be placed on
one axis as if they measured the same

40
00:02:49,213 --> 00:02:49,631
thing.

41
00:02:50,081 --> 00:02:54,247
In this film, each one will be labelled
with its source.

42
00:02:55,747 --> 00:02:58,838
Here is the good news, and it is real.

43
00:02:59,288 --> 00:03:06,009
According to the FAO, deforestation slowed
to ten point nine million hectares per

44
00:03:06,009 --> 00:03:10,738
year between two thousand fifteen and
twenty twenty-five.

45
00:03:11,188 --> 00:03:14,817
In the nineteen nineties it was seventeen
point six.

46
00:03:15,267 --> 00:03:21,309
Net forest loss fell from ten point seven
million hectares per year in the nineteen

47
00:03:21,309 --> 00:03:27,059
nineties to four point one two million in
twenty fifteen to twenty twenty-five.

48
00:03:27,509 --> 00:03:33,191
But ten point nine million hectares a year
is still a very large number.

49
00:03:33,641 --> 00:03:35,510
The trend is falling.

50
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,073
The loss has not stopped.

51
00:03:39,573 --> 00:03:41,588
Now the camera moves closer.

52
00:03:42,038 --> 00:03:48,355
The dashed line is the Legal Amazon, the
area INPE monitors.

53
00:03:48,805 --> 00:03:53,362
Inside and outside it, the colours show
the year of loss.

54
00:03:53,812 --> 00:03:59,836
Every shade on this map was recorded in a
dataset that anyone can download.

55
00:04:00,286 --> 00:04:06,603
INPE publishes the same kind of detail for
the Brazilian Legal Amazon.

56
00:04:07,053 --> 00:04:12,454
Its yearly deforestation layer contains
more than eight hundred thousand

57
00:04:12,454 --> 00:04:16,205
individual polygons, each with a date and
an area.

58
00:04:17,705 --> 00:04:23,986
For the year from August two thousand
twenty-three to July two thousand twenty-

59
00:04:23,986 --> 00:04:30,346
four, INPE estimated six thousand two
hundred and eighty-eight square kilometres

60
00:04:30,346 --> 00:04:31,697
of deforestation.

61
00:04:32,147 --> 00:04:37,465
For the following year, the first estimate
was five thousand seven hundred and

62
00:04:37,465 --> 00:04:39,442
ninety-six square kilometres.

63
00:04:39,892 --> 00:04:45,610
After the data were consolidated in March
two thousand twenty-six, the figure became

64
00:04:45,610 --> 00:04:48,605
five thousand seven hundred and thirty-
one.

65
00:04:49,055 --> 00:04:50,826
Notice that detail.

66
00:04:51,276 --> 00:04:57,328
Even official numbers are revised, and a
careful presentation says which version it

67
00:04:57,328 --> 00:04:57,984
is using.

68
00:04:59,484 --> 00:05:04,286
Satellites also show that the story is not
only about clearing.

69
00:05:04,736 --> 00:05:10,597
Global Forest Watch reported that in two
thousand twenty-four, the world lost six

70
00:05:10,597 --> 00:05:16,603
point seven million hectares of tropical
primary forest, nearly twice as much as in

71
00:05:16,603 --> 00:05:18,484
two thousand twenty-three.

72
00:05:18,934 --> 00:05:25,061
For the first time on record, fires, not
agriculture, were the leading cause, at

73
00:05:25,061 --> 00:05:26,669
nearly fifty percent.

74
00:05:27,119 --> 00:05:33,174
Brazil accounted for forty-two percent of
that loss, and fires caused sixty-six

75
00:05:33,174 --> 00:05:34,707
percent of Brazil's.

76
00:05:35,157 --> 00:05:41,224
In two thousand twenty-five, tropical
primary forest loss fell by thirty-six

77
00:05:41,224 --> 00:05:44,896
percent, to four point three million
hectares.

78
00:05:45,346 --> 00:05:49,659
That is still forty-six percent higher
than a decade earlier.

79
00:05:50,109 --> 00:05:55,828
Across all kinds of tree cover, not only
primary forest, the world lost about

80
00:05:55,828 --> 00:06:01,695
thirty million hectares in two thousand
twenty-four, five percent more than the

81
00:06:01,695 --> 00:06:02,586
year before.

82
00:06:03,036 --> 00:06:09,526
In two thousand twenty-five, global tree
cover loss declined by fourteen percent,

83
00:06:09,526 --> 00:06:12,971
to twenty-five point five million
hectares.

84
00:06:13,421 --> 00:06:17,001
Fires were responsible for forty-two
percent of it.

85
00:06:17,451 --> 00:06:20,689
That is an area larger than the United
Kingdom.

86
00:06:22,189 --> 00:06:24,302
What do these maps tell us?

87
00:06:24,752 --> 00:06:29,505
That forest loss can be measured, mapped,
and published openly.

88
00:06:29,955 --> 00:06:32,899
They also tell us the target is not met.

89
00:06:33,349 --> 00:06:38,361
Global Forest Watch reports that
deforestation in two thousand twenty-five

90
00:06:38,361 --> 00:06:43,982
was seventy percent higher than the level
needed to halt and reverse forest loss by

91
00:06:43,982 --> 00:06:45,337
two thousand thirty.

92
00:06:45,787 --> 00:06:48,340
A map cannot protect a forest.

93
00:06:48,790 --> 00:06:54,130
But it can show us, pixel by pixel,
whether we are keeping our promises.

94
00:06:55,630 --> 00:07:00,921
Every number in this film comes from a
public source, listed on screen.

95
00:07:01,371 --> 00:07:08,732
The sources are the FAO, Global Forest
Watch and the University of Maryland,

96
00:07:08,732 --> 00:07:15,705
INPE, NASA and the U S Geological Survey,
and the European Space Agency.

97
00:07:16,155 --> 00:07:17,095
Thank you.
